{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-7582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/80646a56ea6df17b6f0b6460f3d4918174966773b9e7910d252c9098af3b0f48.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $y=x-3$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=x-3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-3+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-7582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$0.5$ and $4.5$"] ["text" "$1$ and $5$"] ["text" "$1.5$ and $5.5$"] ["text" "$2$ and $6$"] ["text" "$2.5$ and $6.5$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1$ and $5$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/8c9f433d2e23db9dc8abb81395e91acf59047e26221b1218393c4905548d1f35.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ intersects the line $y=x+4$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=x+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x+4-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x-1)^{2} & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =34 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-2,6$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =-2+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-2,2).$ Next, we plug in $x=6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(6,10).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x+4$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(6,10).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,1)$ and $(5,9)$"] ["text" "$(-1,1)$ and $(6,10)$"] ["text" "$(-1.5,1.5)$ and $(5,10)$"] ["text" "$(-2,2)$ and $(5,9)$"] ["text" "$(-2,2)$ and $(6,10)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,2)$ and $(6,10)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/5180dfe04f31f43fdcdfa8bd720951df0c6fb0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$(2,2)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$"] ["text" "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/b29bf5d439b638bfe8ab3fd013467cab8ef7c393a1cd1cf06461fa5807b6c0ba.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=25$ intersects the line $y=-x+1$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=-x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x+1-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1.5$ and $2.5$"] ["text" "$-2$ and $3$"] ["text" "$-2.5$ and $3$"] ["text" "$-2.5$ and $3.5$"] ["text" "$-3$ and $4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7579" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-3$ and $4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d6f30f0e475fc222dc54c4e7f04bc3f48442b8430c3b830bfd7d11a5d6d788e3.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $y-2x=-11,$ as follows: \n\n$$\ny=2x-11\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=2x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-11)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-44x+121 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+130 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+125 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-11 \\\\  & =2(5)-11 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the point $(5,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(4,-1)"] ["math" "(5,-1)"] ["math" "(5,-2)"] ["math" "(6,-1)"] ["math" "(6,-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5,-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/a3a9751a19c9a853c425b81ce63ccb76b4cd4350f152df3dbe00a1cdb0f8cdab.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1.5,-6.5)"] ["math" "(-2,-5)"] ["math" "(-2.5,-3.5)"] ["math" "(-3,-2)"] ["math" "(-4,1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-3,-2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,-1)"] ["math" "(-2,-2)"] ["math" "(-3,-3)"] ["math" "(-4,-4)"] ["math" "(-6,-6)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-7585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-2,-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/43fe48b1980d01aa9dbb7cc0f614272ad19e7cfe47b5a5a83a0f81e7539f285b.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $3y-x=-7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $3y-x=-7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}+5)}^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{16x}{9}+\\frac{64}{9} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $3y-x=-7$ intersects the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ at the point $(1,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,-2)"] ["math" "(1,-3)"] ["math" "(2,-3)"] ["math" "(2,-4)"] ["math" "(3,-5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-15806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=-3x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-3x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-3x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+9{x}^{2}-6x+1 & =1 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10{x}^{2}-4x+1=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $y=-3x.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/87f75953dbe795a8e5db201778b4ff3127f02bae30ef3b8e8ffa7bd7956c1bc3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ and $1$"] ["text" "$-2$ and $2$"] ["text" "$-3$ and $3$"] ["text" "$0$ only"] ["text" "The circle and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The circle and the line do not intersect"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ and $4$"] ["text" "$-1$ only"] ["text" "$-3$ and $-1$"] ["text" "$1$ and $3$"] ["text" "The circle and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-54242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The circle and the line do not intersect"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ intersects the line $2x-y=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $2x-y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+3-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+4{x}^{2}+4x+1 & =4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+12x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={12}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =144-260 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-116<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}+12x+13=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ does not intersect the line $2x-y=-3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/00bc159e9c25b913454c6037a822e41931c6224f3e6f0180da550fe9c857e718.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1.5$ and $3$"] ["text" "$-4$ and $-2$"] ["text" "$-4$ and $2$"] ["text" "$0$ and $4$"] ["text" "The circle and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The circle and the line do not intersect"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ and $3$"] ["text" "$-2$ and $4$"] ["text" "$1$ only"] ["text" "$2$ only"] ["text" "The circle and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The circle and the line do not intersect"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ intersects the line $y=2x+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x+1-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =2 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x+8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-10)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =100-160 \\\\  & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-60<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}-10x+8=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ does not intersect the line $y=2x+1.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/56e313584d7de2f1c40302135b00ef80a1d3c1e920407c9c4a0c62c3c6769456.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ and $-2$"] ["text" "$-1$ and $2$"] ["text" "$2$ and $4$"] ["text" "$3$ and $2$"] ["text" "The circle and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The circle and the line do not intersect"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=13"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\\sqrt{13}"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=52"] ["math" "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=13"] ["math" "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/51d811f0b628a1854542d85954c26ca4658602cfcf7c207fbcfdb41115933139.png)\n\nThe line $4y+x=-8$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)+x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-0 & =-8 \\\\ 4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(-8,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}"] ["math" "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=68"] ["math" "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=17"] ["math" "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=68"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/36e373800d2bd917e38bec197fbb5add2ae785270c68ddb4daf740ad678d4ece.png)\n\nThe line $x+y=2$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, find the equation of the circle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+y & =2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the  coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{2},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2"] ["math" "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4"] ["math" "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2"] ["math" "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2"] ["math" "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-78784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=10"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=40"] ["math" "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=10"] ["math" "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-117851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d627d54450733af4faf74d55497064a4e6ffdc958601862cc49886988f7448c4.png)\n\nThe line $y=2x-4$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =y \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0-4}{2})=(1,-2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,-2)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{5},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "77fb376f7d340934ec3faf82c17732a10c5b300e759fc10fcd21d4a8a0c8d284"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5"] ["math" "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=10"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "q-14670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/state.json" "file_sha256" "93977ed3521daf961eb37204401a9ce3038d278123e336dc1ee0a6d0fdec70b0"} {"question" "e-6761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-6761.json" "file_sha256" "c6bf39788b8651ed6adf6971ab76afa9b333b4a38b7f7c40dad6d56763f9740f"} {"question" "e-6761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = x + 5$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(x+5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+{x}^{2}+10x+25 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+29 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+20 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-2+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-5+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = x + 5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$ at the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/9298e1af177eb9ba1702af0053c03d1d0e696819bae717c4892efdcd574c78fc.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-6761.json" "file_sha256" "c6bf39788b8651ed6adf6971ab76afa9b333b4a38b7f7c40dad6d56763f9740f"} {"question" "e-3234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3234.json" "file_sha256" "9e413fe85497e16ba28ddc144fa6c464bc3e30d156f41106a5e3de8452b8e3e3"} {"question" "e-3234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y = -x + 3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+((-x+3)-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x+10 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+5 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+3 \\\\  & =-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$ at the point $(-1,4).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/90f7e5f187ad79e18d36ce253c8f1f998bc5a92790909f4840c84789c2ae5222.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3234.json" "file_sha256" "9e413fe85497e16ba28ddc144fa6c464bc3e30d156f41106a5e3de8452b8e3e3"} {"question" "e-3235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3235.json" "file_sha256" "56eac52780ef7c7cccfa7773a926b835e2a176f13e73b0ad8f627525db8bf5f3"} {"question" "e-3235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+((3x-1)+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(3x)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+9{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ does not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/590fa0a3fb8cb0e80c46859ad19b9457528c40f79f1ba33edea217d8500b64de.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3235.json" "file_sha256" "56eac52780ef7c7cccfa7773a926b835e2a176f13e73b0ad8f627525db8bf5f3"} {"question" "e-3236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c8ed2363f6899e90eff46d8e45107ed5eee19582aeaed624c9831e5a128ff10a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3236.json" "file_sha256" "0874b601e00f428da06fae6c97171eebe9fa6a97712e5dd55b1ee762e6fc0e22"} {"question" "e-3236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve $-4x+3y=24$ for $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3(0) & =24 \\\\ -4x & =24 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(0)+3y & =24 \\\\ 3y & =24 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(0-(-3))^{2}+(8-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5,$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-3))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/example-3236.json" "file_sha256" "0874b601e00f428da06fae6c97171eebe9fa6a97712e5dd55b1ee762e6fc0e22"}] "usage" {"q-7585" [] "e-6761" [] "e-3234" [] "e-3236" [] "e-3235" []} "basis" 1749 "retractions" [[17592186088263 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/q-7585.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y = x$ intersects the circle $(x + 4)^{2} + y^{2} = 8$."] [17592186087549 :question/problem "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$."] [17592186087549 :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(x + 2)^{2} + (x + 5)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nx^{2} + 4x + 4 + x^{2} + 10x + 25 &= 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 14x + 29 &= 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 14x + 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 7x + 10 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 2)(x + 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2,-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n$y|= x + 5; =-2 + 5; = 3$.\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3)$.\n- Substituting $x=-5$, we get\n$y|= x + 5; =-5 + 5; = 0$.\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the line\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nintersects the circle\n\n$$\n(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9\n$$\n\nat the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0)$.\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/35167f849b7ed3149fb0e1beed852a8f.png)"] [17592186087404 :question/problem "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$."] [17592186087404 :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute\n\n$$\ny = -x + 3\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + ((-x + 3) - 2)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + (-x + 1)^{2} &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 6x + 9 + x^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 8 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x + 10 &= 8 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 5 &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx + 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x + 3 \\\\\n&= - (-1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 3\n$$\n\nintersects the circle\n\n$$\n(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8\n$$\n\nat the point $(-1,4)$.\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/81b24b7386f2be0f994996e137e4dae6.png)"] [17592186087405 :question/problem "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1$."] [17592186087405 :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 1)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((3x - 1) + 1)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + (3x)^{2} &= 3 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 4 + 9x^{2} &= 3 \\\\\n10x^{2} - 4x + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-4)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 16 - 40 \\\\\n&=-24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0\n$$\n\n, there are no real solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3\n$$\n\ndoes not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/36b7751ad0069cf451aaf83b66d1a3ab.png)"] [17592186087406 :question/problem "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/845/Source/Images/9d915606f37edf8b96db4b680ba3cecc.png)"] [17592186087406 :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve\n\n$$\n-4x+3y=24\n$$\n\nfor $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 3(0) &= 24 \\\\\n- 4x &= 24 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4(0) + 3y &= 24 \\\\\n3y &= 24 \\\\\ny &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4)\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{(0 - (-3))^{2} + (8 - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5$, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - (-3))^{2} + (y - 4)^{2} &= 5^{2} \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 4)^{2} &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186086510 17592186086511 17592186086512 17592186086513 17592186086514] "immutable_fields" [[17592186086509 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086509 :answer-field/id] [17592186086509 :answer-field/key] [17592186086509 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
